% =========================================
% ABSTRACT
% =========================================
\begin{abstract}
    Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) streaming algorithms face a fundamental ``plasticity--stability'' dilemma: Reinforcement Learning (RL) methods adapt well but lack stability guarantees, while control-theoretic approaches ensure stability but struggle with complex network dynamics. 
    In this paper, we propose a \textit{Lyapunov-Inspired Future-Aware PPO} agent that bridges this gap. Unlike standard RL that blindly optimizes rewards, our method incorporates a Lyapunov-inspired reward shaping mechanism. Instead of solving computationally heavy Constrained MDPs (CMDP), we inject a virtual queue drift penalty directly into the reward function to implicitly bias the policy toward buffer-safe regimes without adding inference-time overhead. Furthermore, we augment the state space with ``immediate future knowledge'' (next-chunk sizes and scene complexity), enabling the agent to anticipate stochastic throughput drops.
    Extensive experiments on real-world FCC network traces demonstrate that, rather than blindly maximizing raw linear QoE---a metric where QoE-oriented control-theoretic methods such as RobustMPC score marginally higher by operating closer to the buffer depletion boundary---our approach achieves highly competitive visual quality while prioritizing the suppression of catastrophic switching tail-risks. Specifically, it consistently outperforms the pure DRL baseline (Pensieve) by 2.1\% in overall QoE. Furthermore, compared to RobustMPC, it reduces bitrate switching frequency by approximately 70\% while maintaining a near-MPC QoE level with an acceptable stall-risk trade-off. This work demonstrates that integrating lightweight control-theoretic penalties with short-term look-ahead effectively discourages buffer-unsafe actions, significantly enhancing the perceptual stability of neural adaptive streaming.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Adaptive Bitrate Streaming, Deep Reinforcement Learning, Lyapunov Optimization, QoE, Future-Awareness.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

