% SECTION I: INTRODUCTION
% =========================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} exponential growth of video traffic, projected to account for over 82\% of global internet traffic \cite{cisco2023}, has fundamentally transformed the digital landscape. To cope with heterogeneous network conditions and diverse device capabilities, HTTP Adaptive Streaming (HAS) protocols, such as MPEG-DASH and HLS, have become the de-facto standard \cite{sodagar2011mpeg, bentaleb2018survey}. In HAS, a video is segmented into short chunks (e.g., 2-4 seconds), each encoded at multiple discrete bitrate levels. The core responsibility of the client-side player is to dynamically select the optimal bitrate for the next chunk, a decision process known as Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) selection.

The primary objective of an ABR algorithm is to maximize the user's Quality of Experience (QoE) \cite{wu2024qoe}. However, this poses a complex optimization problem involving conflicting goals: (1) maximizing the average visual quality (bitrate/VMAF), (2) minimizing rebuffering events (video stalls due to empty buffer), and (3) minimizing playback instability (frequent quality switching). Achieving an optimal balance remains particularly challenging in modern heterogeneous networks, such as \textbf{mmWave 5G \cite{meng2024mmwave} and Low Earth Orbit (LEO) satellite constellations (e.g., Starlink) \cite{zhang2024satellite}}, where throughput is highly stochastic and exhibits rapid, extreme fluctuations that traditional methods struggle to predict.

Historically, ABR algorithms have evolved through three distinct phases. First, \textit{Fixed-Rule methods} such as Buffer-Based (BBA) \cite{huang2014buffer} and BOLA \cite{spiteri2018bola} relied on heuristic thresholds. While simple, they often fail to utilize available bandwidth efficiently.
Second, \textit{Control-Theoretic methods} like Model Predictive Control (MPC) \cite{yin2015control} framed ABR as a formal optimization problem over a sliding horizon. While MPC provides explicit horizon-based optimization, it does not inherently enforce buffer safety under stochastic throughput, and its performance heavily depends on accurate throughput prediction \cite{sun2016cs2p}. In volatile networks (e.g., 5G or LEO), prediction errors lead to suboptimal decisions---either overly conservative (low quality) or overly aggressive (rebuffering).

Recently, \textit{Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL)} \cite{sutton2018reinforcement} has emerged as a promising paradigm. Systems like Pensieve \cite{mao2017neural} and Oboe \cite{akhtar2018oboe} employ neural networks to learn ABR policies directly from experience, capturing complex patterns without explicit modeling.
However, pure DRL approaches suffer from the \textit{``Plasticity--Stability Dilemma''}. While they are highly plastic (adaptable) to maximizing average reward, they lack inherent stability guarantees. A standard RL agent, driven solely by reward maximization, may learn aggressive behaviors that risk buffer depletion in ``tail'' scenarios, leading to catastrophic stalls. Furthermore, most existing agents are ``future-blind''; they rely solely on past observations and remain unaware of the varying sizes or complexity of upcoming video chunks.

In this paper, we address these limitations by proposing a \textbf{Hybrid Control-RL Framework}. We argue that robust ABR streaming requires bridging the gap between the adaptability of learning-based methods and the safety guarantees of control theory. Specifically, we utilize \textit{Lyapunov Optimization Theory} \cite{neely2010stochastic}, a powerful tool for stabilizing queueing systems, to guide the exploration of a PPO (Proximal Policy Optimization) \cite{schulman2017proximal} agent.
Our specific contributions are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Lyapunov-Inspired Safety Regularization:} We introduce a Lyapunov-inspired penalty derived from queue deficit considerations. Instead of treating rebuffering merely as a negative scalar in the QoE formulation, this mechanism implicitly penalizes unsafe queue deviations. As shown in Theorem 1, this regularization promotes stability through bounded deficit regularization, creating a ``safe zone'' for the RL agent without the computational burden of complex dual-variable CMDP solvers.
    
    \item \textbf{Future-Aware State Space:} We address the ``blindness'' of standard RL by augmenting the state space with immediate future knowledge. By feeding the exact sizes and complexity indices (SI/TI) of the next $M$ chunks into the neural network, we eliminate uncertainty regarding content variations, allowing the agent to distinguish between network-induced and content-induced buffer fluctuations.
    
    \item \textbf{State-of-the-Art Performance:} We conduct extensive trace-driven evaluations using the FCC broadband and Norway HSDPA datasets. Our results demonstrate that the proposed method consistently outperforms Pensieve by 2.1\% in average QoE. More importantly, it significantly enhances perceptual robustness, lowering bitrate switching frequency by approximately 70\% compared to RobustMPC while maintaining near-MPC QoE levels, verifying the efficacy of our risk-aware design.
\end{itemize}

