% SECTION V: EXPERIMENTAL EVALUATION
% =========================================
\section{Experimental Evaluation}
\label{sec:eval}

In this section, we provide a comprehensive evaluation of the proposed Lyapunov-Inspired Future-Aware PPO agent. We aim to answer three key research questions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{RQ1 (Tail-Risk and Overall Performance):} Can a stability-guided RL agent achieve highly competitive QoE while significantly suppressing catastrophic stall risks compared to aggressive optimization baselines?
    \item \textbf{RQ2 (Stability \& Safety):} Can the Lyapunov constraint effectively reduce catastrophic rebuffering events and bitrate oscillations compared to pure RL and control-theoretic methods?
    \item \textbf{RQ3 (Component Efficacy):} What is the individual contribution of ``Future Awareness'' and ``Lyapunov Shaping'' to the final performance?
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{subsec:setup}
\subsubsection{Testbed and Datasets}
We implemented a trace-driven simulator faithful to the MPEG-DASH standard. The environment is built upon \textit{Park's ABR Simulator} and integrated with \textit{Stable-Baselines3} for PPO training.
To ensure robustness, we utilized two distinct network datasets:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{FCC Broadband Dataset:} Comprising over 1 million throughput traces, representing stable but variable conditions. This dataset was used for \textit{training} the RL agent (80\% split).
    \item \textbf{Norway HSDPA Dataset:} A highly volatile 3G/HSDPA dataset characterized by sudden bandwidth drops. This was reserved strictly for \textit{testing} to evaluate generalization to unseen, harsh environments.
\end{itemize}
\subsubsection{Video Content}
We used the reference DASH video \textit{"Enve"} and \textit{"Big Buck Bunny"}, encoded at $\{300, 750, 1200, 1850, 2850, 4300\}$ kbps. The chunk duration is fixed at 4 seconds. For the QoE calculation (Eq. 4), we adopt the standard penalty weights widely used in the literature (e.g., Pensieve): $\lambda = 1$ for switching penalties, and a heavy penalty of $\mu = 4.3$ (scaled appropriately for VMAF) for rebuffering, ensuring a fair and standardized comparison across all baselines.

\subsubsection{Baselines}
We compare our method against widely adopted ABR standard algorithms:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Pensieve \cite{mao2017neural}:} State-of-the-art pure RL (A3C) method.
    \item \textbf{RobustMPC \cite{yin2015control}:} A control-theoretic approach optimizing QoE over a sliding horizon. To ensure reproducibility, we implemented its throughput predictor using the standard Harmonic Mean of the past 5 chunks, as recommended in the original paper.
    \item \textbf{BBA \cite{huang2014buffer}:} A heuristic buffer-based algorithm.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Remark on Comparative Fairness and Information Asymmetry:} A critical reader might argue that providing explicit future MPD metadata (chunk sizes and VMAF) to our agent grants it an unfair ``oracle'' advantage over future-blind baselines like standard Pensieve or RobustMPC. However, identifying how to properly exploit this deterministic future metadata without destabilizing the agent is precisely our core structural contribution. To ensure scientific rigor and isolate the source of our performance gains, we rely on our \textit{Ablation Study (Section V.E)}. As shown later in Fig. \ref{fig:ablation}, simply feeding future knowledge to a standard PPO agent (without Lyapunov regularization) results in massive rebuffering (37.3\%), failing entirely to safely surpass blind baselines. It is exclusively the \textit{combination} of future-awareness and our proposed Lyapunov-inspired safety regularization that unlocks the reported performance, validating that the gain is not a mere artifact of information leakage but the result of a fundamentally safer architectural design.

\subsection{Overall Performance Analysis (RQ1)}
Table \ref{tab:performance} summarizes the mean performance metrics across all test traces. Our proposed agent consistently outperforms the pure DRL baseline, Pensieve (3185.2), by approximately \textbf{2.1\%} with an average QoE of \textbf{3253.2}.

To explicitly address performance under the most volatile network conditions, we evaluated the 5th-percentile QoE and the Conditional Value at Risk (CVaR at 10\%), which represents the average QoE of the worst 10\% of episodes. Traditional RL (Pensieve) suffers a severe 5th-percentile QoE drop to 545.7, while the heuristic BBA collapses to 469.3. By contrast, our Lyapunov-regularized agent maintains a robust 5th-percentile QoE of 598.0, achieving a nearly 10\% improvement in extreme worst-case scenarios over the standard DRL baseline. Aggressive methods like Fugu and RobustMPC achieve marginally higher raw QoE by trading off severe rebuffering (31.62\%) and excessive switching (18.26), respectively.

Crucially, while aggressive methods like RobustMPC and Fugu mathematically yield slightly higher raw Mean QoE scores, they fundamentally achieve this by compromising system safety. For instance, Fugu inherently ``games'' the linear QoE metric by pushing visual quality to the maximum at the catastrophic cost of a 31.62\% rebuffering rate---rendering the stream practically unwatchable. Similarly, RobustMPC relies on severe, erratic bitrate oscillations (18.26 switches per episode). These unsafe strategies inherently degrade the subjective human viewing experience. Our Lyapunov-guided agent effectively suppresses these catastrophic tail-risks, actively ensuring stability and achieving a strictly superior, deployable perceptual trade-off. To ensure statistical rigor, we conducted a Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the episode-wise results, confirming that the 70\% reduction in switching compared to RobustMPC is statistically significant ($p < 0.01$).

