\subsection{Computational Complexity and Overhead}
A critical requirement for client-side ABR algorithms is computational efficiency, as they must execute on resource-constrained devices (e.g., smartphones, smart TVs) without causing battery drain or playback delays.

To empirically validate the deployment feasibility of our approach, we benchmarked the inference time of our proposed Lyapunov-Inspired Future-Aware PPO agent against the standard Pensieve (A3C) baseline. The evaluation was conducted on a commodity CPU mimicking a standard client device.

The computational overhead of our proposed method is remarkably low, driven by two architectural design choices:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Zero Inference-Time Overhead:} The Lyapunov module acts solely as a reward shaper. Because rewards are only utilized during the offline training phase to update policy weights, the Lyapunov-inspired penalty does not introduce any additional computational overhead during online real-time inference.
    \item \textbf{Lightweight Future-Awareness:} Processing future chunk sizes and VMAF scores requires only a small Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) branch. As shown in our benchmarks, the average inference time of our proposed agent is 0.49 ms per chunk, compared to 0.46 ms for the Pensieve baseline. The integration of explicit future-awareness adds a negligible overhead of merely $\sim$0.03 ms. This total decision latency ($<0.5$ ms) is entirely negligible compared to typical network Round-Trip Times (RTT), which generally range from 30 to 80 ms.
\end{enumerate}

Given a standard video chunk duration of $T = 4$ seconds (4000 ms), the decision-making process consumes $\approx 0.01\%$ of the chunk duration. This confirms that our agent delivers substantial QoE and stability improvements without compromising real-time execution, making it highly practical for modern edge devices and web browsers.

